WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:07.389
If you practise criminal law in India, you already know the real work isn't writing a confident paragraph.

2
00:00:07.389 --> 00:00:15.075
It's checking that every part of the offence rests on something in your papers. Pravrudhi-Nyaya helps you do that check.

3
00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:23.627
We're starting with criminal matters. Two names, so nobody gets confused. Pravrudhi-Nyaya is the product you open.

4
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:29.175
Prabhāsa-Nyāya is the set of models we trained ourselves, and they work quietly inside it.

5
00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:38.689
Here's the problem with a general chatbot. It will happily give you a fluent answer. But fluent isn't the same as screened.

6
00:00:38.689 --> 00:00:44.925
A matter needs every ingredient shown, one by one, and each one tied to a fact you can point at.

7
00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:53.750
So you start where you'd start anyway. Paste in the complaint. The page splits it into numbered facts, so each one has a handle.

8
00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.525
Then you tick the offence you want to screen for.

9
00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:08.573
What comes back is a checklist. One line per ingredient of the offence. Each line tells you where it stands, and, just as important, what you should go and check.

10
00:01:08.573 --> 00:01:10.900
That last part is the one to act on.

11
00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:19.003
This one is a public court order, shown as an example only. These are one side's allegations, as the court recited them.

12
00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:26.706
They're not findings, and they're not our view of the case. Notice what the page does when a fact is missing. It doesn't paper over the gap.

13
00:01:26.706 --> 00:01:35.070
It tells you which ingredient still has nothing behind it, and what to look for. And when the two checks disagree, it hands that ingredient back to you.

14
00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:40.848
That's a prompt, not an error. Think of it as a review agenda for the next conversation with your client.

15
00:01:40.848 --> 00:01:45.800
And the page says it plainly: this is a screening aid, not legal advice. A lawyer decides.

16
00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:51.859
Next, the Matter tools page. On Matter tools you add the papers of a matter.

17
00:01:51.859 --> 00:01:58.877
The papers you add here are kept as text in our store, in the EU (Romania), until you delete the paper or the matter.

18
00:01:58.877 --> 00:02:08.775
When you are signed in, Your matters lists your matters, and you can open or delete them there. Deleting removes them from this service's store. Images are not read.

19
00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:14.972
To try the tools without your own papers, load a sample matter.

20
00:02:14.972 --> 00:02:22.600
The sample matter is invented: an invented FIR, chargesheet extract, bail order and cheque memo, with no real parties.

21
00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:35.500
Behind the checklist there are two of our trained models. The first reads an ingredient and names the fact it's leaning on. The second looks again, on its own.

22
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:41.725
Both have to agree. If either one isn't sure, it doesn't guess. That ingredient comes back to you.

23
00:02:43.300 --> 00:02:46.744
Where does a model like that learn? From real court material.

24
00:02:46.744 --> 00:02:54.986
Judgments and orders from real cases, gathered, cleaned up, and read by two separate model readers whose work is compared before anything is used.

25
00:02:54.986 --> 00:03:02.325
Cases we construct ourselves are always labelled as such, and the material we test on is checked against the material we train on.

26
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:07.667
There's one more safeguard that never touches your facts.

27
00:03:07.667 --> 00:03:18.900
Each offence is written down as a precise list of ingredients, and a formal check confirms that every ingredient was actually addressed. So a skipped ingredient shows up.

28
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:28.423
We borrowed three habits from Nyaya, an old Indian tradition of logic. An ingredient only counts when it points to a fact in your papers.

29
00:03:28.423 --> 00:03:36.270
If the facts don't carry it, the system holds back rather than reaching a conclusion. And what a model says is treated like testimony.

30
00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:39.725
It's useful, but it isn't accepted until it's been checked.

31
00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:51.126
Put together, it's a sequence of stages, from reading the facts to the final decision. The last stages decide by fixed rules, not by a model's mood.

32
00:03:51.126 --> 00:03:57.300
And from a result you can download the memo and the run record, to keep with the matter file.

33
00:03:58.800 --> 00:04:08.454
Some firms will want more help than a single screening. So there's an optional layer of managed workflows. The assistant can propose steps, but the core still decides.

34
00:04:08.454 --> 00:04:12.500
It's only for signed-in users, and only when you tick the consent box.

35
00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:20.247
The tools below use those papers, except the Section 138 checklist, which counts from dates you confirm.

36
00:04:20.247 --> 00:04:30.398
Papers for a matter Add the papers of a matter and find a passage in them. Chronology Lists the dates it reads in your papers, each with its sentence. May be incomplete.

37
00:04:30.398 --> 00:04:36.044
Review agenda Lists the ingredients of the cheque-dishonour offence and the papers to look in.

38
00:04:36.044 --> 00:04:41.450
Citation sweep Checks that each citation in your papers exists in our index, nothing more.

39
00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:48.931
Quashing agenda Lists the Supreme Court's quashing categories; does not say whether any applies.

40
00:04:48.931 --> 00:04:54.738
Cheque case check Shows the review agenda and candidate dates per step; you confirm each date.

41
00:04:54.738 --> 00:05:00.360
Hearing pack Gathers the dates, papers still to add and citations to look up for a hearing.

42
00:05:00.360 --> 00:05:06.600
Section 138 checklist Counts section 138 periods from dates you confirm: a checklist, not a decision.

43
00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:15.030
Two tools help with a first draft, for a lawyer to rewrite. Bail application grounds.

44
00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:24.675
Writes a first draft of the grounds from the facts you enter, for a lawyer to rewrite. It is not advice on how the application will go. Synopsis and list of dates.

45
00:05:24.675 --> 00:05:32.426
Shows a list of dates from the papers you added, and a first draft of a synopsis where one can be drafted, for a lawyer to rewrite.

46
00:05:32.426 --> 00:05:39.113
First you say whether any of these is involved in the matter: a person under 18, a sexual offence or trafficking.

47
00:05:39.113 --> 00:05:42.900
If one is, no draft is made and a lawyer drafts the application.

48
00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:53.400
The citation check looks up a citation in our index. Found in our index. It does not look at quotes and does not say whether the case supports a point.

49
00:05:54.900 --> 00:06:03.095
So how is this different from just asking a chatbot? A chatbot can write a fluent answer, and sometimes cite a case that doesn't exist.

50
00:06:03.095 --> 00:06:12.079
Pravrudhi-Nyaya goes ingredient by ingredient, matches quotes against your own facts, and when the facts don't carry it, it refers the point to you.

51
00:06:12.079 --> 00:06:14.750
It's built to be checked, not just believed.

52
00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:25.459
Privacy and where it runs, briefly. Screening without the assistant runs on our own engine and our own models, and your facts aren't sent to an outside AI.

53
00:06:25.459 --> 00:06:32.550
If you choose the assistant, and tick its box, they are, and the page tells you so first. The models run on rented GPUs.

54
00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.400
Pravrudhi-Nyaya. Built to be checked. A lawyer decides.
